.TH UNGETC 3
.SH NAME
ungetc \- push a character back into an input stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

int
ungetc(c, stream)
int c;
FILE *stream;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ungetc
pushes the character
.I c
back onto an input
.IR stream ,
so that the next
.BR getc (3)
returns it. On a buffered stream, one character can always be pushed
back once a character has been read from it. The end of file flag is
cleared.
.PP
.BR fseek (3)
forgets a character that was pushed back.
.PP
A program reading a number, say, stops at the first character that is
not a digit, and pushes it back for the next part of the program to
read.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.IR c ,
or EOF if
.I c
is EOF or the character cannot be pushed back: before anything has
been read from the stream, on a stream with no buffer, or when as many
characters have already been pushed back as were read from the buffer.
.SH SEE ALSO
getc(3), fseek(3), setbuf(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B ungetc
function first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
